1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,289
Welcome to Class 9: Python notebooks for large datasets.

2
00:00:04,289 --> 00:00:08,577
This video introduces the core decisions and working patterns.

3
00:00:08,577 --> 00:00:15,248
Watch the complete lesson first, then use the written class page for copyable commands,

4
00:00:15,248 --> 00:00:17,631
exercises, and detailed reference material.

5
00:00:17,630 --> 00:00:23,581
After this class, you can: start JupyterLab only inside a Slurm allocation;

6
00:00:23,581 --> 00:00:29,532
tunnel the notebook to your workstation without exposing it to the network;

7
00:00:29,532 --> 00:00:35,978
inspect a large dataset by sampling and summarising instead of loading everything blindly;

8
00:00:35,978 --> 00:00:42,425
choose between pandas, Polars, DuckDB, Arrow, NumPy, SciPy, and Matplotlib; distinguish descriptive analysis,

9
00:00:42,425 --> 00:00:50,359
statistical modeling, machine-learning training, validation, and inference; measure memory and runtime; move expensive work from a

10
00:00:50,359 --> 00:00:53,335
notebook into a Slurm batch script.

11
00:00:53,335 --> 00:00:56,259
A notebook kernel is a normal process.

12
00:00:56,259 --> 00:01:00,853
It consumes CPU, memory, local scratch space, and sometimes a GPU.

13
00:01:00,853 --> 00:01:06,283
Therefore, on RCC it must run under Slurm: Read the job output file.

14
00:01:06,283 --> 00:01:11,296
It shows the worker, the selected loopback port, and the tunnel command.

15
00:01:11,296 --> 00:01:15,055
Open only the local address shown by the tunnel.

16
00:01:15,055 --> 00:01:21,321
Do not bind a notebook to a public interface and do not disable the token.

17
00:01:21,321 --> 00:01:28,004
The connection sequence is always: submit the Jupyter job; wait for the job to report its

18
00:01:28,004 --> 00:01:33,434
worker, loopback port, token, and tunnel; run that tunnel command on your workstation;

19
00:01:33,434 --> 00:01:37,193
open the local 127.0.0.1 address; stop the Slurm job.

20
00:01:37,193 --> 00:01:43,570
Use this sequence before writing a full analysis: Describe the question in one sentence.

21
00:01:43,570 --> 00:01:46,303
Inspect the file size and format.

22
00:01:46,303 --> 00:01:50,858
Load a small sample or a small set of columns.

23
00:01:50,858 --> 00:01:52,680
Summarise groups before plotting.

24
00:01:52,680 --> 00:01:54,046
Check memory use.

25
00:01:54,046 --> 00:01:56,323
Save a small reproducible notebook.

26
00:01:56,323 --> 00:01:59,512
Move full-scale work into a Slurm script.

27
00:01:59,512 --> 00:02:03,156
For tabular data, prefer columnar or chunked access.

28
00:02:03,156 --> 00:02:06,800
CSV is portable, but slow for repeated analysis.

29
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:13,632
Parquet, Arrow, DuckDB, or an indexed database table are usually better for repeated interactive work.

30
00:02:13,632 --> 00:02:22,770
The course includes: examples/interactive-workflows/notebooks/python-large-data.ipynb examples/interactive-workflows/python/analysis.py examples/interactive-workflows/python/python.sbatch examples/interactive-workflows/python/environment.yml The notebooks use synthetic data so that you can

31
00:02:22,770 --> 00:02:23,913
practice safely.

32
00:02:23,913 --> 00:02:33,051
Their RiboSnake-inspired section builds a Bray--Curtis PCoA and a ranked waterfall plot in both Python and

33
00:02:33,051 --> 00:02:33,622
R.

34
00:02:33,622 --> 00:02:42,760
Run the cells to render the figures; committed notebook outputs stay empty so that results or

35
00:02:42,760 --> 00:02:46,187
restricted data cannot be published accidentally.

36
00:02:46,187 --> 00:02:53,041
The batch script shows the same idea as a scheduled Slurm job.

37
00:02:53,041 --> 00:02:58,538
Do not paste patient identifiers, tokens, private keys, or passwords into notebooks.

38
00:02:58,538 --> 00:03:02,202
Do not commit notebook outputs containing restricted data.

39
00:03:02,202 --> 00:03:07,241
Keep notebooks small enough that another person can review the reasoning.

40
00:03:07,241 --> 00:03:09,531
Put package versions in environment.yml.

41
00:03:09,531 --> 00:03:13,654
Use project membership rather than sharing another user account.

42
00:03:13,654 --> 00:03:17,777
Shut down the Slurm job when you are finished.

43
00:03:17,777 --> 00:03:24,267
Run the local structure check before using the example on RCC: Then start one Jupyter job

44
00:03:24,267 --> 00:03:29,540
and confirm three things: The job output says the notebook binds to 127.0.0.1.

45
00:03:29,540 --> 00:03:32,379
You can connect through the SSH tunnel.

46
00:03:32,379 --> 00:03:35,218
You can stop the job with scancel.

47
00:03:35,218 --> 00:03:39,680
Do not run more than one notebook job for this class.

